ipcd: set the clock from an NTP server without ntpd

/etc/config/ntp_server never had any effect. timesync.c ran ntpd, but the BusyBox in
this firmware is built without it, so the clock stayed at the year 2000. Adding ntpd
would change the root image, and all cameras would need an update of it.

ipcd now asks the server itself, with a small SNTP client. Before the capture starts it
asks once, which takes 6 seconds at most if nobody answers. A thread then asks every
hour, or every minute until the first answer. It sets the clock if it is half a second
off or more and otherwise lets the kernel correct it slowly with adjtime(). The hardware
clock is written directly, so no child process is involved any more. Without the file
nothing changes: no thread, no traffic.

A reply only counts if it comes from the server, carries our random transmit timestamp
back, and the server says that it is synchronised.

The uptime in /api/stats no longer depends on the clock, which would have added 26 years.

